  "worked_solution": "A function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- The function $f(t)$ is not defined at $t=0$ since its second component $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t$ is not defined at $t=0.$ Hence,  the function $f(t)$ is not differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(t)$  is not defined for $t>3$ and $t<-3,$ since its first component $\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}$ is not defined for these values. Hence,  the function $g(t)$ is not differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{\u2032}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${h}^{\u2032}(t)$ is defined for all $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, we have that   $h(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"$h(t)$ only.\"",
